  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Tricholoma subluteum is a medium-sized woodland mushroom characterized by its yellowish to olive-brown cap and pale gills that may develop yellow staining. This species is found in New England forests and belongs to the Tricholomataceae family, a group known for their terrestrial fruiting bodies.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cap Color and Texture",
      "description": "Yellowish to olive-brown cap with a slightly sticky or dry surface; color may fade with age or moisture",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Gill Characteristics",
      "description": "Pale white to pale yellow gills that are notched near the stem and may show yellow staining or discoloration with handling",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Stem Features",
      "description": "Cylindrical, pale stem with a fibrous texture and possible yellowish tones, especially near the base",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Tricholoma subluteum typically fruits in mixed deciduous and coniferous forests of New England, often appearing near oak, beech, and pine trees during fall months.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The name 'subluteum' refers to its yellowish undertones, and like many Tricholoma species, it may be edible but requires careful identification to avoid confusion with toxic lookalikes.",
